```java
class Bird {
    void fly() { /* flap wings */ }
}
class Sparrow extends Bird {}
class Eagle extends Bird {}

// New requirement: add a Penguin. It's a Bird, but it can't fly.
class Penguin extends Bird {
    @Override
    void fly() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }
}
```

The hierarchy assumed every `Bird` can `fly()`. A `Penguin` breaks that assumption, and the "fix" — overriding `fly()` to throw — is itself a design smell: callers that only know about `Bird` now have to worry it might blow up.

See [composition-vs-inheritance](composition-vs-inheritance) for the full breakdown of this failure mode.

```java
interface MovementBehavior { void move(); }

class FlyingMovement implements MovementBehavior {
    public void move() { /* flap wings */ }
}
class WalkingMovement implements MovementBehavior {
    public void move() { /* waddle */ }
}

class Bird {
    private final MovementBehavior movement;
    Bird(MovementBehavior movement) { this.movement = movement; }
    void move() { movement.move(); }
}

Bird penguin = new Bird(new WalkingMovement()); // no exception, no lie
```

Each `Bird` is composed with the movement behavior it actually has. A `Penguin` never claims to fly, so there's no invariant to violate. This is the **strategy pattern** — see [strategy-pattern](strategy-pattern) for it in full.

Now add diet (`Carnivore`/`Herbivore`) as a second independent axis. With inheritance, covering every combination means a subclass per pair: `FlyingCarnivoreBird`, `WalkingHerbivoreBird`, `FlyingHerbivoreBird`... — the class count multiplies.

```java
class Bird {
    private final MovementBehavior movement;
    private final DietBehavior diet;
    Bird(MovementBehavior movement, DietBehavior diet) {
        this.movement = movement;
        this.diet = diet;
    }
}

new Bird(new WalkingMovement(), new CarnivoreDiet()); // one line, any combination
```

With composition, two independent behaviors stay two small classes — combinations are just constructor arguments, not new subclasses.
